message.content:
Implement the FIELDS module group for a Python-to-Node.js migration.

FIRST: read /workspace/dataset/test11.py so you understand the target program.

YOUR FILES (write exactly these, nothing else):
/output/lib/fields/fieldtypes.mjs, /output/lib/fields/schema.mjs

TEXT/ID/etc. are FACTORY FUNCTIONS returning FieldType instances so that the call syntax
TEXT({stored: true}) mirrors Python's TEXT(stored=True). They must also work with no arguments.
FieldType.index(value) runs the analyzer and returns one tuple per UNIQUE term with its frequency
(and a weight), plus the caller needs the total token count for field length - expose that too
(e.g. return {terms: [...], length: n} from a dedicated method, and keep index() tuple-shaped as
specified; document precisely which method the writer should call).
Coordinate: /output/lib/index/writer.mjs is being written by another agent against the contract, so
keep index()/process_text() signatures exactly as the contract states and make the extra
length-reporting method obvious and defensively usable (writer may call index() only).
IMPORTANT: make FieldType.index() ALSO return length information in a way that cannot break a caller
that just iterates tuples - e.g. attach a non-enumerable/extra property .field_length on the returned
array AND provide word_count(value). Document this in a header comment.
Schema must preserve field insertion order, expose names() sorted the way Whoosh does (Whoosh's
Schema iterates field names in sorted order - state your choice in a comment and be consistent).


=== MEASURED GROUND TRUTH (from running /workspace/dataset/test11_executable) ===
Source: /workspace/dataset/test11.py (read it; it is 26 lines).

Happy path: every valid invocation prints exactly "3\n" to stdout, exit 0.
  ./test11_executable --a hello --b world --c foo         -> "3\n"  exit 0
  ./test11_executable --a "" --b "" --c ""                -> "3\n"  exit 0
  ./test11_executable --a x --b x --c x                   -> "3\n"  exit 0
  ./test11_executable --a=1 --b=2 --c=3                   -> "3\n"  exit 0
  ./test11_executable --a -5 --b 2 --c 3                  -> "3\n"  exit 0   (negative number IS accepted as a value)
  ./test11_executable --a -5.5 --b 2 --c 3                -> "3\n"  exit 0
  ./test11_executable --a=-x --b 2 --c 3                  -> "3\n"  exit 0   (=form accepts any value)
  ./test11_executable --a 1 --a 9 --b 2 --c 3             -> "3\n"  exit 0   (last occurrence wins)
  unicode args also print "3\n" (stdout bytes are exactly 0x33 0x0a)

Error paths: message goes to STDERR, exit code 2, stdout empty. PROG = basename of argv[0].
  (missing one)   --a 1 --b 2
      usage: PROG [-h] --a A --b B --c C
      PROG: error: the following arguments are required: --c
  (missing all)   <no args>
      usage: PROG [-h] --a A --b B --c C
      PROG: error: the following arguments are required: --a, --b, --c
  (unknown opt)   --a 1 --b 2 --c 3 --d 4
      PROG: error: unrecognized arguments: --d 4
  (extra positional) --a 1 --b 2 --c 3 extra
      PROG: error: unrecognized arguments: extra
  (bare double dash) --a 1 --b 2 --c 3 --
      PROG: error: unrecognized arguments: --
  (missing value)  --a 1 --b 2 --c
      PROG: error: argument --c: expected one argument
  (value looks like an option) --a --b --b 2 --c 3
      PROG: error: argument --a: expected one argument
  (value looks like a non-numeric short flag) --a -x --b 2 --c 3
      PROG: error: argument --a: expected one argument
  (wrong-case / undefined short flag) -a 1 --b 2 --c 3   AND   --A 1 --b 2 --c 3
      PROG: error: the following arguments are required: --a
      NOTE ORDERING RULE: the "required" check fires BEFORE the "unrecognized arguments" report.
      So when a required option is missing AND there are leftover unknown args, only the
      required-arguments error is printed.

Help: -h or --help anywhere (even alongside other args, even when required args are missing)
prints to STDOUT and exits 0 with EXACTLY these bytes:
usage: PROG [-h] --a A --b B --c C

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --a A
  --b B
  --c C

(that is: usage line, blank line, "options:", then four two-space-indented entries;
the -h entry has its help text aligned at column 14, i.e. "  -h, --help  show this help message and exit")
For our port, PROG must be derived the way argparse derives it: basename(process.argv[1]) -> "test11.mjs".


=== LOCKED SHARED CONTRACT (other agents are writing the other files RIGHT NOW; do not deviate) ===

HARD RULES (violating any one invalidates the whole deliverable):
 - Pure JavaScript for Node.js. ES Modules ONLY: use import / export.
 - require() and module.exports are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN anywhere, including in comments/strings.
 - Every file has the .mjs suffix. Every relative import MUST include the full .mjs suffix.
 - ZERO external dependencies. No npm packages (no argparse, no yargs, nothing). The ONLY
   non-relative imports permitted are Node builtins written with the node: prefix
   (node:fs, node:path, node:os, node:process, node:url, node:crypto is NOT needed - avoid it).
 - Do NOT shell out to python, do NOT embed python. Do NOT read/extract the executable's internals.
 - No top-level await in library modules (entry may avoid it too; keep everything synchronous).
 - Code must be defensive: never throw on any input the CLI accepts (including empty strings and unicode).

ROOT: /output      ENTRY: /output/test11.mjs      LIBRARY ROOT: /output/lib/

FILE / EXPORT MAP (exact names - other modules import these):

/output/lib/util/py.mjs
  export class PyError extends Error
  export function print(...values)   // Python print(): String() each value, join with ' ', append '\n', write to process.stdout
  export function len(obj)           // Python len(): if obj && typeof obj.__len__ === 'function' -> obj.__len__();
                                     // else string/Array -> .length; Set/Map -> .size;
                                     // else typeof obj.length === 'number' -> obj.length; else throw PyError
  export function repr(value)
  export function isIterable(value)

/output/lib/compat/argparse.mjs
  export class Namespace                      // plain attribute bag; toString like Python Namespace
  export class ArgumentTypeError extends Error
  export class ArgumentParser {
    constructor(options = {})                 // {prog, usage, description, add_help = true, exit_on_error = true}
                                              // default prog = basename(process.argv[1] || '')
    add_argument(...args)                      // e.g. add_argument('--a', {type: String, required: true})
                                              // supports: type, required, default, help, dest, metavar, action ('store','store_true','help')
    parse_args(argv)                           // argv defaults to process.argv.slice(2); returns Namespace
    parse_known_args(argv)                     // returns [namespace, extras]
    format_usage(); format_help(); print_usage(); print_help()
    error(message)                             // prints format_usage() + prog + ': error: ' + message to stderr, exit(2)
    exit(status = 0, message = null)
  }
  MUST reproduce the MEASURED GROUND TRUTH argparse behavior exactly, including the
  required-before-unrecognized ordering rule, the '--opt=value' form, negative-number values,
  the 'expected one argument' error, and the exact help text.

/output/lib/compat/tempfile.mjs
  export function gettempdir()
  export function mkdtemp(options = {})       // {suffix = '', prefix = 'tmp', dir = null}
                                              // creates a directory (mode 0o700) and returns its absolute path

/output/lib/analysis/token.mjs
  export class Token { constructor(fields = {}) }
    // fields/props: text, original, boost, pos, startchar, endchar, stopped,
    //               positions, chars, removestops, mode
    // copy() method
/output/lib/analysis/stopwords.mjs
  export const STOP_WORDS   // a Set. Whoosh's default English stop list, exactly these words:
                            // a an and are as at be by can for from have if in is it may
                            // not of on or tbd that the this to us we when will with yet you your
/output/lib/analysis/tokenizers.mjs
  export const DEFAULT_WORD_PATTERN   // Whoosh's default tokenizer regex, Python-\w-equivalent.
        // Python's \w on str matches [letters, digits, underscore] unicode-aware, so use
        // a unicode-property class such as [\p{L}\p{N}_] with the u flag - NOT bare \w
        // (bare \w is ASCII-only in JS and would mis-tokenize unicode input).
        // Whoosh's default expression is equivalent to: WORD+(\.?WORD+)*
  export class Tokenizer                      // base; has *tokenize(value, opts) generator
  export class RegexTokenizer extends Tokenizer   // constructor(expression = DEFAULT_WORD_PATTERN, gaps = false)
  export class IDTokenizer extends Tokenizer      // whole value as a single token
  export class SpaceSeparatedTokenizer extends Tokenizer
  // Every tokenizer exposes: tokenize(value, {positions=false, chars=false, start_pos=0,
  //   start_char=0, mode='', removestops=true, keeporiginal=false} = {}) -> iterable<Token>

/output/lib/analysis/filters.mjs
  export class Filter                          // base; filter(tokenStream, opts) -> iterable<Token>
  export class PassFilter extends Filter
  export class LowercaseFilter extends Filter
  export class StopFilter extends Filter       // constructor({stoplist = STOP_WORDS, minsize = 2,
                                               //   maxsize = null, renumber = true} = {})
                                               // Whoosh semantics: drops stopwords and tokens with
                                               // text.length < minsize; when removestops is false it
                                               // marks token.stopped = true instead of dropping;
                                               // renumber keeps token.pos contiguous.
/output/lib/analysis/analyzers.mjs
  export class Analyzer                        // base with analyze(value, opts)
  export class CompositeAnalyzer extends Analyzer  // constructor(tokenizer, ...filters)
  export function StandardAnalyzer(opts = {})  // RegexTokenizer + LowercaseFilter + StopFilter({minsize:2})
                                               // opts: {expression, stoplist = STOP_WORDS, minsize = 2, maxsize, gaps}
  export function SimpleAnalyzer(opts = {})    // RegexTokenizer + LowercaseFilter
  export function IDAnalyzer(opts = {})        // IDTokenizer (+ LowercaseFilter if lowercase true)
  export function KeywordAnalyzer(opts = {})
  // CANONICAL analysis entry method name is analyze(value, opts). Every analyzer instance
  // must have .analyze(...). Also attach a .call alias with identical behavior for readability.

/output/lib/fields/fieldtypes.mjs
  export class FieldType {
    constructor({analyzer = null, stored = false, unique = false, field_boost = 1.0,
                 indexed = true, sortable = false, vector = null, spelling = false} = {})
    index(value, opts = {})   // -> array of [text, freq, weight, valueBytes] tuples for one field value
    process_text(value, opts = {})   // -> array of token text strings
    to_bytes(value); self_parsing(); sortable_terms()
  }
  export function TEXT(opts = {})     // returns a FieldType instance (Python TEXT(stored=True) is a
                                      // constructor call, so TEXT({stored: true}) returns an instance).
                                      // Default analyzer: StandardAnalyzer. phrase=true means positions.
  export function ID(opts = {})       // IDAnalyzer, single-token, unique option honored
  export function KEYWORD(opts = {})
  export function STORED(opts = {})   // indexed = false, stored = true
  export function NUMERIC(opts = {})
  export function BOOLEAN(opts = {})
  // Each factory must ALSO work when called with no args: TEXT() / ID().
/output/lib/fields/schema.mjs
  export class Schema {
    constructor(fields = {})          // Schema({title: TEXT({stored:true}), content: TEXT()})
    add(name, fieldtype); has(name); get(name)   // get() throws on unknown field
    names(); stored_names(); scorable_names(); items(); [Symbol.iterator]; copy()
  }
  export class FieldConfigurationError extends Error

/output/lib/index/storage.mjs
  // Real on-disk storage using node:fs sync APIs. Segment/term data serialized as JSON text.
  export class Storage                         // abstract base
  export class FileStorage extends Storage {
    constructor(path, {readonly = false} = {})
    create(); destroy(); folder
    create_file(name); open_file(name)          // open_file returns {readJSON(), readText()}
                                                // create_file returns {writeJSON(obj), writeText(s), close()}
    write_json(name, obj); read_json(name)
    file_exists(name); delete_file(name); list(); lock(name)
  }
  export class RamStorage extends Storage
  export class EmptyIndexError extends Error
/output/lib/index/segment.mjs
  export class Segment {
    constructor(indexname, segid, doccount = 0, deleted = null)
    doc_count(); doc_count_all(); has_deletions(); is_deleted(docnum); delete_document(docnum)
    segment_id(); make_filename(ext)
    toJSON(); static fromJSON(obj)
  }
  export function generate_segment_name(indexname, counter)   // MUST be deterministic (no
                                                              // Math.random / Date.now - they are
                                                              // unavailable/forbidden here): use the counter.
/output/lib/index/writer.mjs
  export class IndexWriter {
    constructor(ix)
    add_document(fields = {})       // fields is an object mapping field name -> value.
                                    // Unknown field name -> throw. Missing fields are fine.
                                    // Builds the inverted index: for each indexed field, run the
                                    // field's analyzer, accumulate term -> {docnum: freq} postings,
                                    // record field lengths, and store stored-field values.
                                    // A document with only empty/stopworded values still counts as a document.
    update_document(fields = {}); delete_by_term(fieldname, text); delete_document(docnum)
    commit(); cancel(); searcher()
  }
export /output/lib/index/reader.mjs
  export class IndexReader {
    constructor(storage, schema, segment, data)
    doc_count(); doc_count_all(); all_doc_ids(); is_deleted(docnum)
    stored_fields(docnum); all_stored_fields()
    has_term(fieldname, text); term_info(fieldname, text); doc_frequency(fieldname, text)
    frequency(fieldname, text); postings(fieldname, text)     // -> matcher over docnums
    field_length(fieldname); max_field_length(fieldname); doc_field_length(docnum, fieldname, def = 0)
    indexed_field_names(); all_terms(); expand_prefix(fieldname, prefix)
    doc_ids_with_field(fieldname)   // docnums having at least one indexed term in that field
    close()
  }
/output/lib/index/index.mjs
  export const TOC_FILENAME_PREFIX, DEFAULT_INDEX_NAME   // 'MAIN'
  export function create_in(dirname, schema, indexname = DEFAULT_INDEX_NAME)  // mkdir -p, returns FileIndex
  export function open_dir(dirname, {indexname = DEFAULT_INDEX_NAME, readonly = false} = {})
  export function exists_in(dirname, indexname = DEFAULT_INDEX_NAME)
  export class FileIndex {
    constructor(storage, {schema = null, indexname = DEFAULT_INDEX_NAME} = {})
    schema (property); storage; indexname
    writer(); searcher(opts = {}); reader(); doc_count(); doc_count_all(); is_empty(); latest_generation(); close()
  }
  export class IndexError extends Error
  export class IndexVersionError extends Error

/output/lib/query/matching.mjs
  export class Matcher { is_active(); id(); next(); score(); weight(); all_ids(); reset(); skip_to(id) }
  export class NullMatcher extends Matcher
  export class ListMatcher extends Matcher     // constructor(ids, weights = null, scorer = null)
  export class UnionMatcher extends Matcher
  export class IntersectionMatcher extends Matcher
/output/lib/query/queries.mjs
  export class Query {
    matcher(searcher)                 // -> Matcher
    docs(searcher)                    // -> array of docnums (ascending)
    estimate_size(reader); normalize(); is_leaf(); field(); with_boost(b); toString(); equals(other)
  }
  export class Term extends Query      // constructor(fieldname, text, {boost = 1.0} = {})
  export class Every extends Query     // constructor(fieldname = null, {boost = 1.0} = {})
      // Whoosh semantics: fieldname null OR '*' -> matches ALL non-deleted documents in the index
      // (this is what the source uses: Every() with no arguments).
      // A concrete fieldname -> only documents that have at least one indexed term in that field.
  export class And extends Query; export class Or extends Query; export class AndNot extends Query
  export class Not extends Query; export class NullQuery extends Query
  export class Prefix extends Query; export class Phrase extends Query
/output/lib/searching/scoring.mjs
  export class WeightingModel { scorer(searcher, fieldname, text, qf = 1) }
  export class BM25F extends WeightingModel    // B = 0.75, K1 = 1.2
  export class Frequency extends WeightingModel
  export class TF_IDF extends WeightingModel
  export function bm25(idf, tf, fl, avgfl, B, K1)
/output/lib/searching/results.mjs
  export class Hit {
    constructor(results, docnum, pos = null, score = null)
    docnum; score; rank; fields(); get(name, def = null); has(name); highlights()
  }
  export class Results {
    constructor(searcher, q, hits, {docset = null, runtime = 0, limit = null} = {})
    __len__()          // Python len(results): the TOTAL number of matching documents in the index,
                       // NOT capped by the search limit. THIS IS WHAT THE SCRIPT PRINTS.
    scored_length()    // number of scored hits actually returned (capped by limit)
    estimated_length(); estimated_min_length(); docs(); docset
    is_empty(); [Symbol.iterator]; hit(i); top_n; score(n); scored_list
    // DO NOT define a property or getter named 'length' on Results - py.len() must route
    // through __len__(). Provide size() as a readable alias for __len__() if you like.
  }
/output/lib/searching/searcher.mjs
  export class Searcher {
    constructor(reader, {weighting = null, schema = null, ix = null} = {})
    reader; schema; doc_count(); doc_count_all(); up_to_date()
    search(query, {limit = 10, sortedby = null, reverse = false, scored = true,
                   optimize = true, filter = null, mask = null, terms = false,
                   groupedby = null, collector = null} = {})   // -> Results
        // limit = null means unlimited. Default limit is 10 (Whoosh's default) but the
        // Results total length must still reflect ALL matching documents.
    search_page(...); documents(kwargs); document(kwargs); document_number(kwargs)
    stored_fields(docnum); postings(fieldname, text); idf(fieldname, text); close()
  }
/output/lib/qparser/parser.mjs
  // The Python source imports QueryParser (unused at runtime) - mirror it faithfully.
  export class QueryParser {
    constructor(fieldname, schema, {group = null, plugins = null} = {})
    parse(text, {normalize = true, debug = false} = {})   // -> Query
        // Support: bare terms, quoted phrases, field:term, AND / OR / NOT / ANDNOT keywords,
        // prefix*, parenthesised groups. Terms are run through the field's analyzer.
    add_plugin(p); remove_plugin_class(c); tag(text); process(text)
  }
  export class SyntaxError_ extends Error
/output/lib/whoosh.mjs
  // Barrel module re-exporting the public surface so the entry can import from one place if
  // desired: fields (Schema, TEXT, ID, ...), index (create_in, open_dir), qparser (QueryParser),
  // query (Every, Term, ...), analysis, searching.

/output/test11.mjs
  // Mirrors /workspace/dataset/test11.py line for line, in the SAME ORDER, with the SAME comments,
  // importing from the library modules the way the Python file imports from whoosh submodules
  // (e.g. import { Schema, TEXT, ID } from './lib/fields/schema.mjs' + fieldtypes, create_in from
  // './lib/index/index.mjs', QueryParser from './lib/qparser/parser.mjs', Every from
  // './lib/query/queries.mjs'). Keep the unused ID / QueryParser imports for 1:1 fidelity.
  // Final statement: print(len(results))  ->  must emit exactly "3\n".


=== SEMANTICS CONSENSUS FROM THE RESEARCH PHASE (use it; flag anything you believe is wrong) ===
--- LENS: Whoosh public-API semantics (searching.Results counting model, query.Every matching model, writer/searcher visibility), applied to /workspace/dataset/test11.py — I read the source of the harness program only; whoosh itself is not installed here and the PyInstaller binary /workspace/dataset/test11_executable was not unpacked. ---
* Default limit of searcher.search(query) [high]: limit=10. The signature is Searcher.search(q, limit=10, sortedby=None, reverse=False, groupedby=None, optimize=True, filter=None, mask=None, terms=False, scored=True). Passing limit=None (or 0, which Whoosh treats as 'no limit') collects every matching document instead of just the top N. test11.py never passes limit, so the effective limit is 10.
  IMPL: With only 3 documents the limit is never binding: 3 <= 10, so the top_n heap holds all 3 hits and no truncation can occur. Changing the default would be invisible in this program unless the document count exceeded it.
* len(results) vs scored_length() vs estimated_length() [high]: len(results) is the TOTAL number of documents matching the query, NOT the number of hits returned, and it is exact and NOT capped by limit. Whoosh computes it lazily: the first len() call forces the full match set to be materialized (an extra pass over the query's doc ids, building results.docset), which is why the docstring warns it can be expensive on large indexes when a limit was used. results.scored_length() is the number of hits actually ranked and held in this Results object (len(results.top_n)) — that IS capped by limit, i.e. min(limit, total_matches), and it is what you can iterate or index into. results.estimated_length() is a cheap UPPER-BOUND sketch from the query tree (q.estimate_size(reader)) rather than from real matching: it does not subtract deleted documents and over-counts compound queries (an AND is estimated from its cheapest child, a NOT is not applied), so it can exceed len(results); it returns the exact value if docset already happens to be loaded. Companions: estimated_min_length() (lower bound) and has_exact_length() (True once docset is loaded). Rule of thumb: scored_length() for 'how many hits do I have in hand', len() for 'how many documents matched', estimated_length() for a fast size sketch.
  IMPL: In test11.py all three collapse to 3: total matches = 3, scored hits = min(10, 3) = 3, and Every().estimate_size(reader) = reader.doc_count() = 3. The printed value therefore cannot distinguish the three APIs, and the line-25 comment '# Get total document count' is correct here only by the coincidence 3 <= 10.
* Every() with no fieldname vs Every('fieldname') [high]: Every() (fieldname None, or the special values '' / '*') matches EVERY document in the index by document number — it enumerates the reader's live doc ids and skips deleted ones. It is content-agnostic: it consults no term index, no postings, and no field values, so a document matches even if it indexed nothing at all. Every('fieldname') is fundamentally different: it matches documents that have at least one indexed term in that field, implemented by walking the field's lexicon and unioning the postings of all its terms. It therefore misses documents where the field was absent, None, stored-but-not-indexed, or analyzed to zero tokens. Both forms score as a constant (the boost) rather than by relevance.
  IMPL: test11.py line 23 uses Every() with no argument — the content-agnostic, all-live-documents form. Switching it to Every('content') or Every('title') is precisely what would make the output input-dependent, since empty and stop-word-only values would then drop out of the match set.
* Do zero-token documents still exist and still match Every()? [high]: Yes to both. add_document() unconditionally allocates a new document number, writes the stored fields, and increments the segment's doc count; producing zero tokens only means no postings are written and the document's field length is 0. Whoosh does not silently drop or reject an all-empty document (empty strings are valid values; only unknown field names raise). reader.doc_count() counts it, and Every() with no fieldname matches it because Every() iterates doc ids, not terms. Such a document is invisible only to term-driven queries: Term, phrase, wildcard/prefix, and Every('thatfield') all skip it. Field-type nuance: whether an empty string yields zero tokens depends on the analyzer. TEXT (RegexTokenizer + StandardAnalyzer) yields nothing for '' and nothing for an all-stop-word value (StopFilter also drops tokens shorter than minsize=2). ID/IDTokenizer-style fields pass the raw value through as one token, so an ID field set to '' can still create an empty-string term — which would matter for Every('id_field') but is irrelevant to Every().
  IMPL: test11.py's schema is title=TEXT(stored=True), content=TEXT — both analyzed TEXT, so all-empty or all-stop-word input genuinely indexes zero terms across the whole index (empty lexicon) while the index still holds 3 documents. No field is required or unique, so there is no dedup/overwrite path (that would need update_document with unique=True).
* Does ix.searcher() after writer.commit() see all 3 documents? [high]: Yes. commit() writes the new segment plus a new TOC generation, and a searcher opened afterwards reads that latest committed generation, so all 3 documents are visible. The program's ordering is the correct one. The classic pitfall is the reverse: Whoosh readers/searchers are point-in-time snapshots, so a searcher obtained BEFORE the commit (or held across it) keeps showing the old state until you call searcher.refresh() / reader.refresh() or open a fresh ix.searcher(); and uncommitted additions are invisible to any searcher. Because all three add_document calls share one writer and one commit, the commit is atomic — you can never observe 1 or 2 documents.
  IMPL: Lines 17-22 are add x3 -> commit -> ix.searcher(), i.e. the safe order. create_in on a fresh tempfile.mkdtemp() also guarantees a virgin index with no leftover segments or deletions, so doc_count() == doc_count_all() == 3.
* Reconciliation: why the measured output is always exactly 3 [high]: The four answers compose into an input-invariant constant. (a) Three add_document calls plus one commit always yield exactly 3 live documents, regardless of what the strings analyze to, because document creation is not conditional on token production. (b) Every() with no fieldname matches by document number and never touches the term index, so all 3 match even when the entire lexicon is empty (all-empty-string or all-stop-word input). (c) The default limit=10 exceeds 3, so nothing is truncated. (d) len(results) reports total matching documents = 3, coinciding here with scored_length() = 3 and estimated_length() = 3. The program is therefore a constant function of its inputs: for every (--a, --b, --c) it prints '3'. The measurement is not an artifact of the sampled inputs — no string value can change it. The levers that would introduce input sensitivity are code-level, not input-level: use Every('content')/Every('title') or a parsed term query (empty and stop-word-only values then vanish), index more than 10 documents and print scored_length() instead of len(), delete documents before searching, or use update_document with a unique field so duplicate values collapse.
  IMPL: Corollary for any differential or fuzzing use of this program: stdout carries zero bits of input information, so divergence can only surface via crashes/stderr/exit status or at the argv layer, never via the printed number. Do not read '3' as evidence that a given input was actually indexed with terms.
RISKS: Precise internals of Results.__len__ (lazily building results.docset and re-walking the query, versus a cached count) are recalled from Whoosh 2.x public docstrings, not verified against source here. The observable contract (exact total matches, not capped by limit) is solid; the mechanism description is the softer part. | Whether Every('') and Every('*') are treated identically to Every(None) is version-sensitive. If a given Whoosh version differs, only the fieldname-bearing variants are affected, not the no-argument call used in test11.py. | The claim that IDTokenizer emits an empty-string token for an empty value is a nuance I could not verify in this environment. It does not affect test11.py (ID is imported on line 3 but never used), so do not rely on it when reasoning about other schemas. | estimated_length() equaling 3 here depends on Every.estimate_size returning reader.doc_count(); a version returning doc_count_all() could exceed len() once deletions exist. Irrelevant for a freshly created index with no deletions. | Untested crash-shaped input classes that would change stderr/exit status rather than the printed number: a single enormous token (one multi-kilobyte word with no whitespace) exercising term-key length encoding in the on-disk term/hash files, and unpaired surrogates or otherwise undecodable text reaching the tokenizer. Both are speculative and were not exercised. | argv-level hazards sit outside the program's logic but still change observed behavior: a value beginning with '-' or '--' passed as a separate token makes argparse fail with exit code 2 instead of printing 3. Use --a=VALUE form or '--' separation when fuzzing. | Environment caveat: whoosh is not importable here (confirmed by a failed import) and the PyInstaller binary was deliberately not unpacked, so none of these answers were executed. They rest on public-API knowledge plus reading /workspace/dataset/test11.py.

--- LENS: Whoosh analyzer-chain recall, calibrated by confidence, then Python→JS Unicode/regex fidelity for the port. Items 1-6 are from memory of Whoosh's public docs/API (no Whoosh installed in /workspace — confirmed `ModuleNotFoundError`), so each carries an explicit confidence and a one-line verification command. Item 7 is not recall: I empirically tested Python 3 `re` and Node against the same inputs, so its claims are measured, not remembered. ---
* 1. Default analyzer for whoosh.fields.TEXT [High — this is the canonical documented chain and the three-link shape is stable across Whoosh 2.x.]: StandardAnalyzer(). Exact chain, in order: RegexTokenizer(expression=default_pattern, gaps=False) | LowercaseFilter() | StopFilter(stoplist=STOP_WORDS, minsize=2, maxsize=None). StandardAnalyzer is a composition function, not a class — it returns a CompositeAnalyzer of those three links. The StopFilter link is conditional: StandardAnalyzer(stoplist=None) returns only RegexTokenizer | LowercaseFilter. Note there is NO stemming — that is StemmingAnalyzer (RegexTokenizer | LowercaseFilter | StopFilter | StemFilter), which TEXT does not use by default.
  IMPL: Port as three composable stages over a token stream, not one regex pass. Also mirror TEXT's other defaults: phrase=True (positions recorded, so your token objects need pos) and no field_boost change. Keep the stages separately swappable — the stoplist=None case must drop the third stage entirely rather than pass an empty set, because an empty stoplist still applies minsize.
* 2. Default RegexTokenizer / StandardAnalyzer pattern [High on the pattern text; high on the dot-bridging behavior (verified by running that exact pattern under Python 3 re in this session).]: r"\w+(\.?\w+)*" — exposed publicly as whoosh.analysis.tokenizers.default_pattern (a precompiled pattern). Semantics: one or more word chars, then zero or more repetitions of (optional single dot + one or more word chars). Consequences: "www.example.com" and "1.5" survive as single tokens; a sentence-final dot in "end." is NOT consumed (the tail requires word chars after the dot), so you get "end"; "a..b" yields "a" and "b" (only a single dot may bridge). gaps=False by default means the regex matches the tokens themselves, not the separators — gaps=True inverts it to re.split behavior.
  IMPL: Do not 'simplify' this to \w+ during the port. The dot-bridging is load-bearing for URLs, decimals, and version strings, and dropping it silently changes which documents match. Convert the capturing group to non-capturing in JS: (?:\.?...)* — the group is never read, and leaving it capturing costs you on every match.
* 3. whoosh.analysis.STOP_WORDS contents [MEDIUM — this is the weakest item in this report and the one most likely to be off by a word or two. Treat the list as a hypothesis, not a spec.]: A frozenset of ~34 short words. My belief, complete list: a, an, and, are, as, at, be, by, can, for, from, have, if, in, is, it, may, not, of, on, or, tbd, that, the, this, to, us, we, when, will, with, yet, you, your. Two things I am confident about structurally even if a word or two is off: (a) it is deliberately TINY — ~34 entries vs ~179 in NLTK's English list — so it is NOT a general-purpose English stoplist; (b) it contains the distinctly non-English-prose entry 'tbd', a fingerprint of Whoosh's origins as a documentation/help indexer. Notably ABSENT (do not add them): was, were, been, but, they, he, she, his, her, all, would, there, what, which, who, how, do, no, so, than, then, these. StopFilter also accepts lang=, which pulls a much larger per-language list from whoosh.lang.stopwords instead.
  IMPL: Verify before porting, do not trust me: `python -c "from whoosh.analysis import STOP_WORDS; print(len(STOP_WORDS)); print(sorted(STOP_WORDS))"` and paste the literal output into the JS source as a frozen Set. A wrong stoplist is the worst class of porting bug here because it fails silently — no error, just different recall on a subset of queries, and it corrupts index and query symmetrically so round-trip tests still pass. Any 1-character entry you think you see is redundant anyway (minsize=2 already removes it).
* 4. StopFilter minsize in StandardAnalyzer, and what minsize does [High on minsize=2 and maxsize=None; high on the strictly-less-than boundary (2-char tokens are kept); slightly lower on renumber=True being the default, though I believe it is.]: minsize=2 (and maxsize=None, i.e. no upper bound). minsize removes any token whose text length is strictly less than minsize, so the default drops all 1-character tokens. It is a pure character-length test applied after LowercaseFilter, entirely independent of the stoplist — a token is dropped if it is in the stoplist OR shorter than minsize OR (if maxsize set) longer than maxsize. Practical consequence people trip on: single letters and single digits are unsearchable in a default TEXT field — "Vitamin C", "Plan B", "Section 5", "C" all lose that token. StopFilter also defaults to renumber=True, which closes the positional gaps left by removed tokens, so phrase queries spanning a removed stopword behave as if the stopword were never there.
  IMPL: Subtle JS trap: Python len() counts code points, JS .length counts UTF-16 code units. An astral-plane single character (e.g. U+1D400, or many CJK extension chars) is len 1 in Python — dropped — but .length 2 in JS — kept. Use [...token].length (or a code-point counter) for the minsize/maxsize comparison, never .length. Conversely, decomposed 'é' is len 2 in Python, so NFD text can slip past minsize in Python where NFC text would not — another reason to fix normalization explicitly (see item 7).
* 5. Default analyzer for whoosh.fields.ID, and "the quick brown fox" [High — this whole-value-as-one-term behavior is the entire point of the ID field type.]: IDAnalyzer(), which is just a bare IDTokenizer() (it appends a LowercaseFilter only if you pass lowercase=True). IDTokenizer emits the entire field value as exactly ONE token, verbatim. So "the quick brown fox" tokenizes to a single token: "the quick brown fox" — spaces preserved, case preserved, no stopword removal, no minsize filtering, position 0. It is therefore findable only by the exact whole-string term, e.g. ID:"the quick brown fox"; searching ID:quick matches nothing. (IDAnalyzer is documented as deprecated in favor of using IDTokenizer directly, but ID still defaults to it.) Empty string yields zero tokens.
  IMPL: In JS this is a one-line stage: yield a single token with text = the raw value, pos 0, startChar 0, endChar = value.length. Do not route ID values through your text pipeline's trim/lowercase helpers — the case and internal whitespace sensitivity is intentional and is what makes ID usable as a primary key with unique=True. If your port needs case-insensitive IDs, that must be an explicit IDAnalyzer(lowercase=True) equivalent, mirrored on the query side.
* 6. TEXT(stored=True) vs TEXT() [High.]: stored only controls retrievability, never searchability. With stored=True, Whoosh saves the ORIGINAL pre-analysis value in the document's stored-fields record, so it comes back in results (hit["body"], hit.fields(), searcher.stored_fields(docnum)). With the default stored=False, the field is still fully indexed, searched, and scored identically — you just get a KeyError / missing key when you try to read it off a Hit, because the value was never kept. What stored does NOT change: the analyzer chain, the terms written to the index, scoring, or matching. What it costs: index size (a full copy of the text). What it enables: displaying the text, and highlighting — whoosh.highlight needs the original text, which it normally takes from the stored field (otherwise you must pass the text in yourself, or add term vectors).
  IMPL: Keep the stored copy in a separate per-document blob keyed by docnum, distinct from the inverted index, so the two concerns stay independently optimizable. Preserve the important detail that the stored value is the raw input, NOT the analyzed/lowercased/stopped tokens — reconstructing display text from the token stream is lossy and is a classic port regression. Whoosh also supports overriding the stored value via a _stored_<fieldname> key in add_document; decide up front whether your port needs that escape hatch.
* 7. Exact JS regex reproducing Python's \w for tokenization, + bare-\w failure mode [High — measured in this session against both Python 3 re and Node, not recalled.]: USE: /[\p{L}\p{N}_]+(?:\.?[\p{L}\p{N}_]+)*/gu

Flags: g (iterate all matches via matchAll/exec, matching re.finditer) and u (REQUIRED — it is what enables \p{...} and switches to code-point semantics so astral-plane letters match as one character). Do NOT add i: the tokenizer is case-sensitive and lowercasing is a separate downstream filter. Add d only if you want explicit indices; matchAll already gives m.index, and endChar = m.index + m[0].length. The ES2024 v flag works in place of u if you prefer set notation.

WHY that character class is exact: Python documents str-mode \w as 'alphanumeric characters (as defined by str.isalnum()) as well as the underscore'. I measured the actual category coverage: é(Ll) 世(Lo) ʰ(Lm) ٣(Nd) ½(No) Ⅷ(Nl) all match; U+0301 combining acute (Mn), U+203F undertie (Pc), and emoji (So) all do NOT. So Python \w == \p{L} ∪ \p{N} ∪ {ASCII underscore} exactly — note \p{L} excludes _ (it is Pc), so _ must be listed literally, and other Pc chars must NOT be added.

FAILURE MODE with bare \w on "héllo 世界" (measured, Node): /\w+(?:\.?\w+)*/g yields ["h", "llo"] — 'é' is not a word char so the word is shredded at the accent — and "世界" produces ZERO matches, vanishing entirely. Then LowercaseFilter no-ops and StopFilter(minsize=2) drops "h", so the document indexes as the single meaningless term ["llo"]. The correct chain yields ["héllo", "世界"], both length>=2, both kept. Diagnostic framing: JS bare \w reproduces Python's re on BYTES, not str — I ran rb'\w+(\.?\w+)*' against the same input and got exactly [b'h', b'llo'] with the CJK gone. Porting with bare \w silently runs your tokenizer in bytes mode.

Why it is insidious rather than loud: (a) a CJK/Thai/Arabic document is inserted into the index but produces no terms, so it exists and matches nothing — zero recall, no exception; (b) the same broken analyzer runs on QUERIES, so searching "世界" produces an empty token list and an empty query that returns no hits rather than erroring, meaning index/query stay symmetrically wrong and ASCII-fixture round-trip tests keep passing; (c) the surviving fragments are worse than nothing — "llo" collides across unrelated accented words, so you get false positives alongside the false negatives; (d) accented ASCII-language text (French, Spanish, Vietnamese) degrades partially and plausibly, which reads as a relevance-tuning problem rather than a tokenizer bug. And critically: adding the u flag does NOT fix \w — unlike Python's re.UNICODE or PCRE's /u, JS \w stays [A-Za-z0-9_] under u and v. I verified /\w+/gu still splits 'héllo' into h/llo.
  IMPL: Combining-marks decision, which you must make consciously: [\p{L}\p{N}_] is FAITHFUL to Python — I confirmed Python splits NFD 'héllo' into ['he','llo'] and so does this class. Adding \p{M} ([\p{L}\p{N}\p{M}_]) produces the BETTER answer ['héllo'] but diverges from Whoosh. Recommended: keep the faithful class and instead NFC-normalize with value.normalize('NFC') at both ingest and query time — that fixes decomposed input without changing behavior on composed input. Exception: if you must read or write an existing .whoosh index built by Python, do NOT normalize, because Whoosh has no normalization step and your terms would stop matching. Also mind that JS lacks re.finditer's guarantees around lastIndex — reuse of a /g regex across calls is stateful, so construct it per-call or reset lastIndex. \w+ cannot match empty so there is no zero-length-match infinite loop here, but preserve that property if you ever make the pattern more permissive.
RISKS: ITEM 3 IS THE ONE TO DISTRUST. The STOP_WORDS list is medium confidence and I may be off by a word or two. It is also the highest-blast-radius error because it fails silently and symmetrically (same wrong list on index and query), so tests pass while recall differs. Gate the port on running `python -c "from whoosh.analysis import STOP_WORDS; print(len(STOP_WORDS)); print(sorted(STOP_WORDS))"` and hardcoding the literal output. If len() != 34, my list is wrong. | Whoosh version drift: everything above reflects Whoosh 2.x as commonly documented. There is no Whoosh installed in /workspace, so I could not pin a version. If the source project uses whoosh-reloaded or a 3.x fork, default_pattern, STOP_WORDS, and the StandardAnalyzer chain are all plausible divergence points — check the actual installed package, not PyPI docs. | Silent-failure class in general: every default here (stoplist membership, minsize=2, the dot-bridging in the pattern, \w's Unicode scope) changes WHICH DOCUMENTS MATCH without ever raising. None of these produce a crash or a type error. Build a golden-corpus test (index N documents, assert the exact sorted term list per field) rather than relying on unit tests that round-trip through the port's own analyzer — a round-trip test cannot detect a symmetric analyzer bug. | Non-ASCII fixtures are mandatory in the test corpus. An ASCII-only test suite passes 100% with the bare-\w bug present. Include at minimum: CJK ('世界'), an accented Latin word in both NFC and NFD forms, an Arabic-Indic digit ('٣'), an astral-plane letter, an underscore identifier, a URL, and a decimal — those seven inputs discriminate every regex/normalization/length decision in item 7. | UTF-16 vs code-point counting leaks beyond the regex into minsize/maxsize (.length vs [...s].length) and into startChar/endChar offsets. JS offsets are self-consistent in UTF-16 units, but they are NOT comparable to Python character offsets for text containing astral characters. If highlighting or any stored offset crosses the Python/JS boundary, the spans will silently point at the wrong characters. | LowercaseFilter is not a trivial port either: Python str.lower() and JS toLowerCase() are both full Unicode case mappings but disagree on edge cases (e.g. U+0130 'İ', Cherokee, some length-changing mappings). This can make a term indexed by Python unfindable by the JS query analyzer even when the tokenizer is correct. Test the lowercase stage independently. | Not verified in this session and worth confirming from the real package: StopFilter's renumber=True default (affects phrase-query positions across removed stopwords), whether TEXT's phrase=True default is unchanged in your version, and the exact `_stored_<fieldname>` override behavior. I stated these with lower internal confidence than the numbered answers they sit inside. | Scope caveat: this covers only TEXT and ID as asked. KEYWORD (commas=False, lowercase=False, scorable=False), NGRAM/NGRAMWORDS, and NUMERIC each have their own defaults that will bite separately, and NUMERIC in particular does not use an analyzer at all.

--- LENS: CPython 3.12.3 argparse internals, read from /usr/lib/python3.12/argparse.py and validated by differential fuzzing (312 arg vectors, curated + random, with and without COLUMNS) between /workspace/dataset/test11_executable and a hand-written JS reference port at /tmp/port/test11.mjs — 0 byte-level mismatches on stdout, stderr, and exit code. ---
* Version identification (do this first) [high]: The bundled interpreter is CPython 3.12.x, matching /usr/lib/python3.12/argparse.py exactly. Two fingerprints confirm it: (a) help heading is 'options:' (3.10+), and (b) a trailing bare '--' is REPORTED as 'unrecognized arguments: --' rather than being silently dropped (fixed in later 3.12.x/3.13). Also 3.12-specific: help invocation renders '-h, --help' (not the 3.13 '-h, --help' variant with shared metavar) and _get_values only strips '--' for positionals (`if not action.option_strings and action.nargs not in [PARSER, REMAINDER]`).
  IMPL: Do not port from memory of a different Python. Every behavior below was verified against /usr/lib/python3.12/argparse.py and the binary.
* Q1 — Is a '-'-leading token an option or a value? (_parse_optional, exact 9-step order) [high]: argparse classifies EVERY token once, up front, into 'O' (option) or 'A' (value/positional) via _parse_optional (argparse.py:2287-2333). Steps in order: (1) token === '' -> A. (2) token[0] not in prefix_chars ('-') -> A. (3) exact hit in _option_string_actions ('-h','--help','--a','--b','--c') -> O, explicit_arg=null. (4) token.length === 1 (i.e. bare '-') -> A. (5) split at FIRST '=': if prefix-before-'=' is a known option string -> O with explicit_arg = rest. (6) _get_option_tuples abbreviation search (allow_abbrev default True): for '--xxx' tokens, every registered option string that startsWith(prefix-before-'=') is a candidate; for '-x' tokens, candidates are (a) exact 2-char short prefix match with the tail as explicit_arg, and (b) options that startWith the whole token. >1 candidate -> error 'ambiguous option: TOK could match A, B'; exactly 1 -> O. (7) _negative_number_matcher = /^-\d+$|^-\d*\.\d+$/ : if it matches AND _has_negative_number_optionals is empty -> A. (8) token.includes(' ') -> A. (9) otherwise -> O with action=null ('looks like an option, unknown to this parser').

Why '-5' is a value but '-x' is not: both fail steps 3-6. At step 7, '-5' matches ^-\d+$ and this parser registers no option string that itself looks like a negative number, so _has_negative_number_optionals stays [] and '-5' is reclassified as a VALUE ('A'). '-x' matches neither alternation branch, contains no space, so it falls to step 9 and becomes an unknown OPTION ('O'). Since --a's value pattern only accepts 'A' (see Q2), '--a -x' then fails with 'expected one argument'.

_has_negative_number_optionals: populated in _ActionsContainer._add_action (argparse.py:1533-1535) — it gets [True] only if some registered option_string itself matches the negative-number regex (e.g. add_argument('-1')). With options -h/--help/--a/--b/--c it is permanently []. So the port can hardcode 'no negative-number optionals'.

Measured boundary cases (all verified): '-5' A, '-05' A, '-0' A, '-9999999999' A, '-5.5' A, '-.5' A, '-.0' A  ==> accepted as values; '-5.' O, '-1e5' O, '--5' O ('^-\d+$' allows only ONE leading dash), '-x' O ==> 'expected one argument'. '-x y' and ' -x' are values (space rule / not-prefix rule). '-' alone is a value (step 4). '' is a value (step 1).
  IMPL: JS: const NEG = /^-\d+$|^-\d*\.\d+$/; test the RAW token. Note ^-\d*\.\d+$ requires digits AFTER the dot and allows none before, so '-.5' passes and '-5.' fails. Classify all tokens BEFORE consuming anything; the classification never changes later.
* Q2 — 'expected one argument' vs 'unrecognized arguments' (the pattern-match rule) [high]: After classification, argparse builds a string arg_strings_pattern, one char per token: 'O' for options, 'A' for values, and '-' for a literal '--' token (argparse.py:1969-1990). Then _get_nargs_pattern(action) yields a regex: for nargs=None it starts as '(-*A-*)' BUT the last two lines (argparse.py:2415-2417) do `if action.option_strings: pattern = pattern.replace('-*','').replace('-','')` — so for an OPTIONAL with nargs=None the pattern is exactly '(A)'. For the help action (nargs=0) it becomes '()'.

Rule: in consume_optional (argparse.py:2020-2098), for an option with no explicit '=' argument, argparse regex-matches '(A)' against arg_strings_pattern.slice(i+1) anchored at the start. If the very next token was classified 'A' it is consumed as the value; otherwise _match_argument raises ArgumentError -> 'argument --X: expected one argument' (exit 2). So: 'expected one argument' fires when the option is RECOGNIZED but the token immediately after it is not classified 'A' (it is 'O', or '-', or the option is last). 'unrecognized arguments' fires only at the very END, in parse_args, for every token that ended up in the `extras` list — i.e. tokens classified 'O' whose action is null (unknown options), and tokens classified 'A' that no positional could absorb (this parser has zero positionals, so ALL leftover 'A' tokens go to extras).

Equivalently, for this parser: 'expected one argument' = a local, immediate, per-option failure raised during left-to-right consumption; 'unrecognized arguments' = a deferred, global, end-of-parse report. The immediate error always wins if it occurs earlier in the scan (e.g. '--a -x --d 4' reports 'argument --a: expected one argument', never the --d extra).

The action name in the message is _get_action_name(action) = action.option_strings.join('/') (argparse.py:52-63 + ArgumentError.__str__). For --a/--b/--c that is '--a'/'--b'/'--c'; for the help action it is '-h/--help' — so '--help=x' errors as "argument -h/--help: ignored explicit argument 'x'". This was a real bug in my first port; the message never uses the option string the user typed.
  IMPL: Implement literally: `if (pattern[i+1] !== 'A') die(`argument ${NAME[action]}: expected one argument`)` where NAME maps action -> option_strings.join('/'). Keep one `extras` array appended to in original order and only report it after the required-check.
* Q3 — Why '-a 1 --b 2 --c 3' says 'required: --a' (parse_known_args / parse_args split + ordering) [high]: parse_args (argparse.py:2427-2432) is a thin wrapper:
  args, argv = self.parse_known_args(...); if argv: self.error('unrecognized arguments: %s' % ' '.join(argv)); return args
All of the real work — including the REQUIRED check — happens INSIDE _parse_known_args, which returns (namespace, extras). The required check is at argparse.py:2168-2188, i.e. AFTER extras have been collected but BEFORE _parse_known_args returns. So parse_args never gets a chance to report extras when something required is missing: parser.error() calls sys.exit(2) from inside _parse_known_args.

Trace for '-a 1 --b 2 --c 3': '-a' -> step 6 finds no candidate ('-h' and '--help' don't startWith '-a'; '--a' starts with '--', not '-a'), not a negative number, no space -> step 9 -> classified 'O' with action=null. consume_optional sees action===null, pushes '-a' to extras, returns i+1. '1' is classified 'A'; with zero positionals consume_positionals consumes nothing, so '1' is also pushed to extras. --b and --c bind normally. At the required check, seen_actions lacks the --a action -> required_actions = ['--a'] -> error 'the following arguments are required: --a', exit 2. extras (['-a','1']) is discarded. Identical for '--A 1 ...' (matching is case-sensitive; nothing startsWith '--A').

ORDERING RULE, exhaustive and confirmed by fuzzing: (1) errors raised token-by-token during consumption (expected one argument / ignored explicit argument / ambiguous option) — earliest offending token wins; the -h/--help action also FIRES here, printing help and exiting 0; then (2) required-arguments check, joining missing names with ', ' in the order the actions were ADDED (so '--a, --b, --c', never sorted or input order); then (3) mutually-exclusive group check (n/a); then (4) unrecognized arguments, joined with a single ' '.
  IMPL: Structure the port as: classify -> consume loop (may exit here) -> required check -> extras check -> print '3\n'. Never merge steps 2 and 4.
* Q4 — Usage string generation and optional ordering [high]: HelpFormatter._format_usage (argparse.py:296-402) with prefix='usage: '. Actions are partitioned by `if action.option_strings` into optionals and positionals, then _format_actions_usage(optionals + positionals) — optionals ALWAYS first, each group in the order the arguments were ADDED. add_help=True registers -h/--help first (in ArgumentParser.__init__, before any user add_argument), so the order is -h, --a, --b, --c.

Per action: metavar defaults to _get_default_metavar_for_optional(action) = action.dest.upper(). dest for '--a' is 'a', so metavar is 'A'. nargs=None -> args_string is just the metavar. part = optionstrings[0] + ' ' + args_string. If nargs==0 (help), part = optionstrings[0] alone. Then `if not action.required and action not in group: part = '[' + part + ']'`. -h is not required -> '[-h]'; --a/--b/--c are required=True -> bare, NO brackets.

Result: action_usage = '[-h] --a A --b B --c C', usage = prog + ' ' + action_usage, and format_usage() returns 'usage: ' + usage + '\n\n', which format_help() then normalizes (collapse 3+ newlines to 2, strip leading/trailing newlines, append exactly one '\n'). print_usage therefore emits exactly: 'usage: test11.mjs [-h] --a A --b B --c C\n'.

Wrapping (only if 'usage: '.length + usage.length > text_width, text_width = width - current_indent = width): opt_usage is re-split with /\(.*?\)+(?=\s|$)|\[.*?\]+(?=\s|$)|\S+/g, which yields SEVEN parts ['[-h]','--a','A','--b','B','--c','C'] — note '--a A' splits into two independent parts, so a wrap can land between an option and its metavar. If 'usage: '.length + prog.length <= 0.75 * text_width, indent = ' '.repeat(7 + prog.length + 1) and lines = get_lines([prog, ...optParts], indent, prefix) with lines[0] having the indent sliced off; else prog goes on its own line with indent = ' '.repeat(7). get_lines is greedy: line_len starts at (prefix ?? indent).length - 1, and a part is pushed to a new line when line_len + 1 + part.length > text_width and the current line is non-empty; line_len += part.length + 1.
  IMPL: For prog='test11.mjs' the one-line form is 40 chars and never wraps at the default width 78; wrapping only matters if COLUMNS < 42. Implementing get_lines verbatim is ~15 lines and makes the port width-exact — I verified byte equality at COLUMNS 25/30/38/45/60/80/200.
* Q5 — prog derivation [high]: ArgumentParser.__init__ (argparse.py:1799-1801): `if prog is None: prog = os.path.basename(sys.argv[0])`. sys.argv[0] is the script path as invoked. For the frozen binary that is './test11_executable' -> 'test11_executable'. For the JS port, sys.argv[0] maps to process.argv[1] (the script path; process.argv[0] is the node executable), so PROG = basename(process.argv[1]) = 'test11.mjs'. prog is used verbatim in the usage line and as the 'PROG: error: ...' prefix.
  IMPL: Use Python's basename semantics, not node's: `const p = process.argv[1] ?? ''; const PROG = p.slice(p.lastIndexOf('/') + 1);`. path.basename('/x/y/') returns 'y' in node but '' in Python — the manual slice matches Python. Compute PROG once at startup and never hardcode 'test11.mjs'.
* Q6 — '--opt=value' splitting; is '--a=-x' accepted? [high]: Splitting happens in _parse_optional step 5 via str.partition('='), which splits at the FIRST '=' only: arg_string.partition('=') -> (before, '=', after). If `before` is a registered option string, the tuple (action, before, '=', after) is returned, so explicit_arg = everything after the first '=' — it may itself start with '-', contain '=', or be empty.

In consume_optional, when explicit_arg !== null, argparse calls _match_argument(action, 'A') — it forces the literal pattern 'A', completely bypassing token classification. Since --a's pattern is '(A)', arg_count is 1 and the value is taken as-is. Therefore '--a=-x' IS accepted (args.a === '-x'), '--a=--b' is accepted, '--a=1=2' gives '1=2', '--a=-5' gives '-5', and '--a=' gives the EMPTY STRING '' — which still marks --a as seen, so required is satisfied ('--a= --b 2 --c 3' prints 3).

Contrast the other branch: if arg_count were 0 (nargs=0 option like --help) and an explicit arg was given, argparse raises "ignored explicit argument 'X'" — e.g. '--help=x' -> "argument -h/--help: ignored explicit argument 'x'". For a single-dash zero-arg option with an attached tail (e.g. '-hx'), argparse instead treats it as bundled short options: it takes the help action (which prints help and exits 0) and would push '-x' to extras.

Also note step 3 (exact match) runs BEFORE the '=' split, and abbreviation (step 6) also honors '=': '--he=1' resolves to --help with explicit_arg '1'.
  IMPL: Split with indexOf('=') and require the index > 0; take everything after it, do not re-split. Never re-classify explicit_arg — it is always accepted verbatim for a nargs=None option.
* Q7 — bare '--' in this version [high]: In the classification loop (argparse.py:1974-1978) the FIRST token equal to '--' appends '-' to the pattern and then drains the remaining iterator appending 'A' for every subsequent token — so nothing after '--' is ever an option, and additional '--' tokens become plain 'A's. Crucially, in 3.12.3 the '--' token itself is NOT removed from arg_strings: _get_values only strips '--' for POSITIONALS (`if not action.option_strings and ...`, argparse.py:2511-2516), and _get_nargs_pattern strips all '-'/'-*' from optionals' patterns, so an optional can never absorb a '--'.

Consequence for a parser with zero positionals: the '--' and everything after it survive to `extras` and are reported verbatim. Verified: '--a 1 --b 2 --c 3 --' -> 'unrecognized arguments: --'; '... -- x' -> 'unrecognized arguments: -- x'; '... -- --' -> 'unrecognized arguments: -- --'; '--a 1 -- --b 2' -> 'unrecognized arguments: -- --b 2' (note --b after '--' is NOT recognized, so --b/--c go missing only if they hadn't already been supplied — the required check still runs first). And '--a -- 5' -> 'argument --a: expected one argument', because the token after --a has pattern '-', not 'A'.
  IMPL: Port literally: on the first token === '--', push '-' then push 'A' for every remaining token and stop the classification loop. Do NOT delete '--' from the token array (that is the later-Python behavior and would produce 'unrecognized arguments: ' with an empty tail or silently succeed).
* Q8 — Help output formatting (exact columns) [high]: HelpFormatter.__init__ (argparse.py:173-192): width = shutil.get_terminal_size().columns - 2 (COLUMNS env if it parses to >0, else the tty size, else the 80 fallback when stdout is not a tty -> width 78 under test). Then _max_help_position = min(24, max(width - 20, 4)).

Invocations from _format_action_invocation (argparse.py:583-607): nargs==0 -> option_strings.join(', ') = '-h, --help' (10 chars); otherwise each option string is rendered as 'OPT METAVAR' and joined with ', ' -> '--a A' (5). _action_max_length = max(invocation length) + current_indent(2) = 12.

Per-entry geometry (argparse.py:530-570): help_position = min(_action_max_length + 2, _max_help_position) = min(14, 24) = 14; help_width = max(width - help_position, 11) = 64; action_width = help_position - current_indent - 2 = 10.
  - If action.help is falsy (--a/--b/--c have no help): header = ' '.repeat(2) + invocation + '\n'. No padding at all -> '  --a A\n'.
  - Else if invocation.length <= action_width: header = ' '.repeat(2) + invocation.padEnd(action_width) + '  ' (indent_first = 0). '-h, --help' is exactly 10 = action_width -> '  -h, --help' + '  ' = help text starts at column 14.
  - Else header = ' '.repeat(2) + invocation + '\n' and indent_first = help_position (this is what happens at narrow widths, where _max_help_position shrinks).
  Help text is whitespace-collapsed then textwrap.wrap(text, help_width); line 0 is prefixed with indent_first spaces, continuation lines with help_position spaces.

Section assembly in format_help: usage block, description (None), then each action group as a section titled 'positional arguments'/'options' — a section whose body is empty renders as '' (so no 'positional arguments:' heading appears) — then epilog. Finally 3+ consecutive newlines collapse to 2, the string is stripped of leading/trailing newlines, and exactly one '\n' is appended.

Exact expected bytes (width 78), confirmed via od -c against the binary:
'usage: PROG [-h] --a A --b B --c C\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help  show this help message and exit\n  --a A\n  --b B\n  --c C\n'
  IMPL: Help goes to STDOUT with exit 0; usage+error go to STDERR with exit 2 (print_usage(stderr) then 'PROG: error: MSG\n'). The help action fires the instant its token is consumed, so '--help --a', '--a 1 --help', '--h', '--he', '-hx' and '--z --help' all print help and exit 0 even with required args missing or unknown extras present — but '--a --help' does NOT: --a's 'expected one argument' error is raised first because it is the earlier token. Do not describe help as unconditionally winning.
* Extra — value binding, type=str, repeated options, and the program body [high]: nargs=None + action='store' -> _get_values takes arg_strings[0] and applies _get_value (type=str is the identity, and no ArgumentTypeError path is reachable), then setattr overwrites the namespace slot, so the LAST occurrence wins ('--a 1 --a 9' -> '9') and required is satisfied by the first occurrence. Empty-string values are fine and count as 'seen'. Unicode passes through unchanged. The Whoosh body is value-independent: it always adds exactly 3 documents and prints len(results) for an Every() query, and Whoosh's default search limit is 10 >= 3, so successful runs always emit exactly the two bytes 0x33 0x0a. No argument value can change that, including '' and non-ASCII.
  IMPL: On the happy path just `process.stdout.write('3\n')` — a single write, no trailing blank line. args.a/b/c never need to reach the search logic for the count to be 3, but do keep them if the port implements Whoosh faithfully.
* Extra — validated reference implementation [high]: A complete, byte-exact JS reference port lives at /tmp/port/test11.mjs (copies at /tmp/port/test11_executable and /tmp/port/test11_executable.mjs used for name-length-exact diffing). It implements: _parse_optional's 9 steps, the O/A/'-' pattern string, consume_optional with the '(A)'/'()' patterns, explicit-arg handling including the nargs=0 'ignored explicit argument' and short-option-bundling branches, abbreviation with ambiguity detection, extras collection, the required-then-extras ordering, _get_action_name's '/'-join, the full usage get_lines wrapper, and the help geometry with _max_help_position = min(24, max(width-20, 4)). Differential harnesses: /tmp/diff.py (52 curated cases), /tmp/fuzz2.py and /tmp/fuzz3.py (random token vectors with COLUMNS variation). Final tallies: 52/52 and 260/260 with zero mismatches on (stdout, stderr, exit code).
  IMPL: Two bugs my first attempt hit, both worth guarding against in the real port: (1) treating -h/--help as needing a following 'A' token (its nargs is 0, pattern '()', so it must bind zero args and fire immediately); (2) using the typed option string instead of option_strings.join('/') in ArgumentError messages, which breaks '--help=x' -> must say '-h/--help'.
RISKS: Python-version drift is the single biggest trap. In later CPython (3.12.7+/3.13) the bare '--' is stripped from extras, _format_action_invocation renders '-h, --help' differently, and _get_nargs_pattern/_get_values differ. The measured 'unrecognized arguments: --' pins this binary to 3.12.3-era behavior. Port from /usr/lib/python3.12/argparse.py, not from memory or newer docs. | Help/usage output is terminal-width dependent: width = shutil.get_terminal_size().columns - 2, which honors the COLUMNS env var. The stated ground-truth bytes correspond to width 78 (stdout piped, no COLUMNS). If a grader runs under a narrow terminal or sets COLUMNS, both usage wrapping and the help column shift (_max_help_position = min(24, max(width-20, 4))). Implement the width logic rather than hardcoding column 14, or the port diverges outside width 78. | prog length feeds the wrapping decision, and the port's prog ('test11.mjs', 10 chars) differs from the binary's ('test11_executable', 17 chars). At very narrow widths the two will legitimately wrap differently; this is unavoidable and not a port bug. Only compare wrap behavior using equal-length prog names. | '-h/--help' vs '--help' in ArgumentError messages is easy to get wrong and is only exercised by '--help=x' / '-h=1' style inputs, which a thin test suite may miss while a fuzzer will not. | The claim 'help anywhere always wins' in the task brief is not exactly true: '--a --help' errors with 'argument --a: expected one argument' because per-token consumption errors fire in left-to-right order before the help action is reached. Encoding 'scan for -h/--help first, then parse' would produce a wrong result for that input. | Zero positionals is what makes extras equal 'all unconsumed tokens in original order'. If anyone later adds a positional to the port, the real interleaved consume_positionals / _match_arguments_partial machinery (and the '--' stripping inside _get_values for positionals) becomes necessary; the simplification silently breaks. | Byte-exactness of stdout matters: '3\n' only, and help must end with a single trailing newline after '  --c C'. console.log-style double newlines or a BOM would fail an od -c comparison. Likewise error output must go to stderr with stdout completely empty. | Abbreviation is on by default (allow_abbrev=True), so '--h', '--he', '--hel' all resolve to --help. A port that only does exact matching would report these as unrecognized instead of printing help.

